Description: framework: services act on each eye of a frame of two eyes
 A frame of two eyes (stereo3d_unpack's in a 3D project: both views side by
 side, the left eye first, marked stereo3d.pair) is given to every filter and
 transition one eye at a time, as a picture of its own, so what a service
 places, moves, scales, crops, rotates or blurs stays in that eye and is
 measured in that eye's samples; the services keep the profile of one eye. The
 framework wraps the entries a service's process pushes on the frame
 (mlt_filter_process, mlt_transition_process): services stacked directly on
 one another act on each eye as one chain, so what one leaves on the frame for
 the next (mask_start for mask_apply) belongs to the same eye; the picture
 under them is taken once, for both eyes, at twice the eye's width; a
 transition's second frame is taken once and each eye of the transition gets
 that eye of it (or the whole of it when it holds one picture); each eye after
 the first starts from the frame's properties as the services found them (a
 qtblend leaves its scaling for the next qtblend) plus what taking the picture
 under them set; while a service is set up (its process) the frame says one
 eye's media width (crop sizes itself then). A producer's normalizers and
 stereo3d_unpack itself act on the frame as a whole; so do services that keep
 earlier pictures (freeze, temporal blends and denoisers, deinterlacers,
 stabilizers, trackers), listed in mlt_stereo_eyes.c and marked on the service
 by the factory as _stereo3d.whole_frame: 1 when that is the same as each eye
 apart, 2 when it can carry part of one eye into the other (an application
 tells the person). The tractor keeps the mark on its frame. A frame without
 the mark, every 2D project's, takes the code paths it always took.
 .
 stereo3d_unpack: in_producer with pair_frame=1 makes a frame of two views each
 of the profile's size (twice its width), so a render of a 3D project keeps
 the profile of one eye, its consumer asking for the doubled width with a
 profile of its own; every frame it makes side by side carries
 stereo3d.pair; a clip whose own frame already is that frame (both views side
 by side, left first, each filling its half) is handed on as the producer gives
 it, in the format asked for and at its depth. Asked for a frame of another size
 (a service acting on each eye asking for a smaller picture), it scales each
 eye apart to its half of that size with MLT's rescaler, as a 2D producer's
 normalizers would, so no scaler reaches across the middle.
